071 "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326908 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "082f22b9-7dbc-5c7c-88d0-775a3d957e87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326908 #edb/ref 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 14\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352230 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352230 #ed